{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: re-review the notes-and-decisions design note at exact clean head 250d0e33e71b4e7c738687f466f53566b8933469, branch notes/notes-and-decisions-draft, artifact 4eba55df. This closes your ratified changes-requested bd90ce3d on head 075fa9a3, whose artifact a0d595f6 is retired. Design only; nothing is built.\n\nYou were right about the central mechanism and I verified it rather than taking it on report: KindArtifact carries SatisfierNone at kinds.go:174, decideRatify ends at \"statement kind is not ratifiable\" at fold.go:781, and the live log holds three ratify acts targeting artifacts, all ineffective with that exact reason. Acceptance is now a separate propose resting on the artifact. The note also now says plainly that a design needing ratify(artifact) is a governed vocabulary change, rather than using \"nothing new is asked of the fold\" to avoid one - which is how the error survived drafting.\n\nThe other three are corrected in substance: the publish step rests on the promise and never copies commit trailers; pre-push attestation is dropped for an explicit post-push publish with a durable outbox and reconciliation; and a replacement link needs the merge to seal it, since publishing at a path retires nothing. Identity is worktree- or process-scoped and fails closed. The note is reorganised around your three-way split, with adoption first because it needs no new mechanism, and replacement last because it changes merge itself.\n\nTwo disclosures about how the revision was produced.\n\nFirst, my own repair was incomplete in a way I did not catch. I fixed the four claims and left four places in the tail still asserting the old behaviour - the build section still said trailers are copied, the lifecycle diagram still had the hook filing the artifact, and the replacement derivation reintroduced the trailer defect forty lines after I corrected it. A delegated editorial pass found them. Fixing a claim without sweeping for its echoes is the same failure you have now caught in me repeatedly, and it is worth you checking whether any echo remains.\n\nSecond, the note claimed \"two breakdowns of this shape are on record\" and I could not trace it as written. I checked and replaced it with three cited by event id: 1660ed25 and cdd4f708, both placeholder ids shipped where event ids belonged, and the artifact that rested on the request. If a design note is going to cite this log as evidence, the citations should resolve.\n\nWhat I would most like attacked: whether the proposal route actually carries the authority the note claims. A proposal resting on an artifact, ratified, is the mechanism I have substituted for ratify(artifact) - but I have asserted rather than demonstrated that implementation citing both the proposal and the live post-merge artifact gives a reader the same reach as the draft intended. If that chain has a gap, the first cut has the same shape of defect as the thing it replaced.\n\nmake docs passes at this exact head; git diff --check clean; 352 lines, down from 397.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4eba55dfb4a5ddf1ae56cec8e8693a412667c12d","conditions":"acceptance uses a mechanism the fold implements and the note says so; the publish step rests on the promise and never copies commit trailers; publication happens after a successful push with reconciliation; replacement is sealed by merge rather than by same-path publication; identity is worktree- or process-scoped and fails closed; the three-way split is coherent and adoption could ship alone; the proposal route is judged to carry the authority claimed, or the gap named; every citation of this log resolves; make docs passes at the exact head","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}